summary:
Mission Ignis is an interactive web app that turns NASA’s split satellite fire record into one consistent history and a forecast. MODIS (1 km pixels, since 2000) and VIIRS (375 m, since 2012) count fire very differently: in Southeast Asia, VIIRS reports 4.7 times as many hotspots for the same fires. So raw trends break whenever the satellite mix changes.

I matched individual detections between the sensors, then calibrated MODIS and Suomi NPP onto the NOAA-20 VIIRS scale, region by region and season by season. Every year was tested with a model that never saw it: accuracy 0.96, with an 80% error band that held 79% of the time.

The result is one record from 2001 to 2026 across 8 regions worldwide. Users can explore it on a 3D globe, select any country or province, and download it.

On top of the record:
- a live view of the past seven days;
- a 26-week forecast using El Niño and NASA POWER rainfall;
- a haze outlook for Malaysia and Singapore;
- season timing;
- a burn-window planner.

This gives fire agencies, haze forecasters, farmers and researchers a trustworthy answer to one question: is this season really worse than before?
